HOME_ROADMAP_16 = [
("best-water-softener-for-your-home", "understand",
"Water softeners: the signs you need one and the honest choices",
"Limescale on everything, soap that won't lather, laundry like cardboard - how to confirm hard water, and which of the three system types fits your house.",
"""<p>Hard water - water carrying dissolved calcium and magnesium - does not harm you, but it quietly taxes everything it touches: heating elements, pipes, glassware, towels and your Saturday mornings. Here is how to confirm it and choose the fix.</p>
<h2>Confirm it first (ten minutes, a few pounds)</h2>
<p>The signs are a constellation: white chalky crust on taps and kettle elements, soap that barely lathers, spots on glassware out of the dishwasher, stiff laundry, dry-feeling skin and hair, and a showerhead slowly petrifying. Test strips or a small titration kit from a hardware shop give you the actual hardness number - measure before buying anything, because systems are sized to it and some homes with "hard-feeling" water turn out mild.</p>
<h2>The three system types, honestly</h2>
<p><b>Salt-based ion exchange (true softening).</b> The classic whole-home system: hardness minerals are swapped for a trace of sodium, regenerated with salt you refill every month or two. It genuinely softens - soft water lathers richly and stops new limescale. The costs are real: floor space, salt purchases, a drain connection, and drinking water that carries a small amount of sodium (worth knowing if anyone is on a sodium-restricted diet - a separate drinking tap or point-of-use filter covers that neatly).</p>
<p><b>Salt-free conditioners.</b> These do not remove hardness; they change how the minerals behave so they form less scale on surfaces and heating elements. Lower maintenance and no salt, but the water is still technically hard - expectations set correctly, they are a legitimate scale-reduction play, not a softener.</p>
<p><b>Point-of-use reverse osmosis.</b> An under-sink unit for drinking and cooking water only - excellent water, zero effect on the shower or the limescale problem. It pairs with a softener rather than replacing one.</p>
<h2>The buying and running rules</h2>
<p>Size by household and measured hardness - an undersized system regenerates constantly and an oversized one wastes salt; a reputable installer calculates it from your test result. Whole-home softening is plumbing work: this is the pro line for most owners, and quotes should include the drain and the bypass valve. And soften with open eyes: no system makes water "healthier" - it makes it kinder to pipes, machines and soap. Descale what the years already left - <a href="/home/how-to-descale-a-kettle/">the kettle method is here</a> - and note that hard water feeds the film on washer door seals - <a href="/home/washing-machine-mould-door-seal/">that routine is here</a> - and the sediment layer in heater tanks - <a href="/home/water-heater-flush-how-to/">the annual flush matters double in hard-water homes</a>.</p>"""),

("thermostat-settings-that-save-money", "maintain",
"The thermostat settings that actually save money",
"The DOE-backed numbers for winter and summer, the setback trick worth up to 10% a year, and the heat-pump caveat nobody mentions.",
"""<p>The cheapest home-upgrade of the year costs nothing: three thoughtful numbers on the thermostat. Here are the settings energy agencies actually recommend - and the one equipment type that breaks the rules.</p>
<h2>The anchor settings</h2>
<p>The US Department of Energy's long-standing guidance: <b>winter</b>, about 68°F (20°C) when you are home and awake; <b>summer</b>, about 78°F (26°C) when home. UK and EU guidance runs the same logic in Celsius - heat to around 18-21°C in lived-in rooms. These are comfort-plus-efficiency anchors, not laws: adjust a degree at a time until your household agrees, and remember sweaters are free.</p>
<h2>The setback trick - the real money</h2>
<p>The bigger saving is not the daytime number; it is the <b>setback</b>: letting the house drift 7-10°F (about 4-6°C) cooler in winter - or warmer in summer - for the roughly eight hours nobody is home or everyone is asleep. Done consistently, the DOE puts the saving at as much as 10 per cent of annual heating and cooling costs. Every additional degree of setback saves a little more; the total depends on your climate, insulation and how long the setback runs - which is why honest advice gives ranges, not promises. A schedule you set once and forget beats heroic daily adjustments - <a href="/home/smart-thermostat-payback/">a smart thermostat automates exactly this, and the payback maths is here</a>.</p>
<h2>The heat-pump caveat</h2>
<p>Deep setbacks can backfire on heat pumps: recovering the temperature quickly can trigger less-efficient backup (resistance) heat, eating the savings. Many modern heat-pump thermostats manage this intelligently - if your home runs on a heat pump, use its scheduling modes rather than big manual swings. This is also a "check your manual, or ask the installer" moment rather than a rule-of-thumb moment.</p>
<h2>The rest of the money is in the house</h2>
<p>The thermostat sets the target; the house decides how hard the system works to hit it. Drafts, thin loft insulation and blocked vents inflate every hour of runtime - <a href="/home/condensation-ventilation-that-works/">healthy ventilation keeps comfort honest</a>, <a href="/home/hvac-filter-sizes-and-merv/">a fresh filter is the one-minute maintenance win</a>, and <a href="/home/standby-power-real-numbers-your-meter/">the small always-on loads are worth a meter check too</a>. Set the schedule this evening; the savings start with the very first night.</p>"""),

("repair-or-replace-appliances", "understand",
"Repair or replace? The appliance maths that settles it",
"The 50% rule, the typical lifespans that frame it, and the energy arithmetic that quietly tips the decision - so the next breakdown is a calculation, not a panic.",
"""<p>An appliance dies at the worst possible moment, and the sales floor knows it. A few rules of thumb turn the panic purchase into a five-minute calculation.</p>
<h2>The 50% rule</h2>
<p>If the repair quote is more than half the price of a comparable new unit, <i>and</i> the machine is past the halfway point of its expected life, replacement usually wins. Both halves matter: a £180 repair on a three-year-old washer is reasonable; the same repair on a twelve-year-old one is a down payment on the next failure.</p>
<h2>Typical lifespans - the honest ranges</h2>
<p>These vary by brand, usage and water hardness, but the commonly cited ranges are: washing machines roughly 10-12 years, dryers around 12-13, dishwashers 9-10, fridges 10-15 (compressor models stretching further with clean coils), ranges and ovens often 13-15+ (the simplest machines last longest). A machine at half its range with one failed part is a repair candidate; one at the far end of range with a failed control board rarely is - <a href="/home/smart-appliances-worth-it/">and replacement is the moment to rethink the "smart" upsell</a>.</p>
<h2>The energy arithmetic people forget</h2>
<p>Older appliances - fridges above all - can cost visibly more to run than modern equivalents, so replacement pays a second dividend on the electricity bill. Be honest though: the saving is meaningful mainly when replacing genuinely old, inefficient units, not a working five-year-old machine. If you keep the old one, the cheap maintenance that extends life is boringly effective - <a href="/home/fridge-coils-twice-a-year/">fridge coils twice a year</a>, <a href="/home/washing-machine-mould-door-seal/">the door-seal routine</a>, <a href="/home/vacuum-cleaner-care-guide/">the vacuum's filter-and-brush haircut</a>.</p>
<h2>Repairs that are almost always worth it</h2>
<p>Simple mechanical failures on otherwise-healthy machines: hoses, belts, door seals, thermal fuses, a blocked pump, a snapped roller. If the fix is a part and an hour, repair wins at almost any age - <a href="/home/washing-machine-wont-drain/">many washer "failures" are a filter check away</a>, and <a href="/home/how-to-defrost-a-freezer-properly/">some "broken" freezers are just frost</a>.</p>
<h2>Before you decide anything</h2>
<p>Get the quote first - diagnosis fees are usually rolled into the repair if you proceed. Dig out the model number and the receipt; check the warranty twice before paying anyone. And keep a one-line note per appliance of its age and any past repairs - the next breakdown then takes minutes to price, not a weekend of dread.</p>"""),

("air-purifier-worth-it", "understand",
"Air purifiers: what HEPA actually means and when one is worth it",
"The filter definition that matters, the room-size rule, the honest wins (smoke, dander, pollen) - and the one type to never bring home.",
"""<p>Air purifiers sold briskly through every smoke season and pandemic scare - and half the marketing around them is fiction. Here is what the machines genuinely do, in plain definitions.</p>
<h2>The definition that matters: HEPA</h2>
<p>True HEPA - "high efficiency particulate air", a standard defined by the US Department of Energy - means the filter captures at least <b>99.97 per cent of particles at 0.3 microns</b>. That size is not a gimmick; it is the hardest particle size to catch, and both larger and smaller particles are trapped even more efficiently. Labels saying "HEPA-type" or "99%" carry no such guarantee - the two-word difference is the whole product. Note what HEPA does <i>not</i> do: it traps particles, not gases - odours and volatile chemicals need an activated-carbon layer, which good units include and cheap ones skip.</p>
<h2>Sizing: the spec that decides everything</h2>
<p>A purifier is only as good as its fit to the room. The number to check is <b>CADR</b> - clean air delivery rate - which combines filtration and airflow; the practical rule is matching the unit's rated room size to (or above) your actual room, and remembering that claims assume the unit runs for hours with the door closed. One small purifier "for the whole house" purifies the corner it sits in.</p>
<h2>When a purifier genuinely earns its plug</h2>
<p><b>Smoke days</b> - wildfire smoke has made this the tier-1 use case, and a HEPA unit in the bedroom is the measured winner there. <b>Pet dander and pollen seasons</b> - it reduces airborne particles; allergy sufferers often notice bedrooms most. <b>Dusty city flats</b> with windows that cannot stay open. It pairs with - not replaces - the boring wins: source control and ventilation - <a href="/home/condensation-ventilation-that-works/">the ventilation basics are here</a> - and for central systems, filtration at the air handler: <a href="/home/hvac-filter-sizes-and-merv/">MERV 13 is the EPA-level recommendation if your system supports it</a>.</p>
<h2>The type to never buy</h2>
<p>Avoid anything whose selling point is <b>ozone</b> - "energised oxygen", "fresh-air" ionisers that emit it deliberately. Ozone is a lung irritant with no safe indoor level; regulators are unambiguous on this. If the box promises to clean the air by reacting with it, leave it on the shelf.</p>
<p>The honest verdict: one true-HEPA unit, correctly sized, running in the room that matters most (usually the bedroom), is a legitimate buy for smoke, pets and pollen - <a href="/home/bathroom-fan-condensation/">while the bathroom's moisture problem, like every source problem, belongs to the fan, not the purifier</a>.</p>"""),

("sump-pump-failure-signs", "maintain",
"Sump pumps: the failure signs and the ten-second test",
"The pump that keeps the basement dry fails quietly, then loudly during the worst storm of the year. The warning signs, the water test, and the backup plan.",
"""<p>A sump pump is the least-appreciated appliance in houses with basements: it sits in a pit below the floor, waits for groundwater to rise, and quietly ejects it before your storage room becomes a pond. When it fails, it fails at the worst time - which is why a ten-second test each season is the whole maintenance story.</p>
<h2>The test every few months</h2>
<p>Pour a bucket of water slowly into the sump pit. The float should rise and switch the pump on; the pump should eject the water briskly and shut off cleanly. That is the entire check - and it catches the two most common failures (a stuck float and a dead motor) before the storm does. Do it before spring thaw and before the wet season, every time.</p>
<h2>The failure signs, in order of urgency</h2>
<p><b>Runs constantly or cycles every few minutes</b> - either a float stuck or a check valve letting water flow backwards; a pump that never rests is a pump that dies early. <b>New noises</b> - grinding, rattling or clunking signal a worn impeller or debris in the pit. <b>Visible rust or a greasy film</b> on the pit water - corrosion is on the timetable. <b>Age</b> - these are consumables; commonly replaced on the order of every ten years, sooner if it works hard. <b>Vibration when running</b> - a bent or damaged impeller.</p>
<h2>The two things people forget until water</h2>
<p><b>Power cuts.</b> Heavy storms take the electricity down exactly when the pump is needed most - a battery backup (or a water-powered backup where plumbing allows) is the upgrade that separates a damp story from an insurance claim. <b>The discharge line.</b> The pipe that carries water away must stay clear and sloped - crushed, frozen or disconnected lines quietly undo the pump's work; check where it empties too, well away from the foundation.</p>
<p>The rest of the dry-basement system: know <a href="/home/how-to-shut-off-water-main/">where the water main shutoff is</a> for burst-pipe days, keep a cheap water alarm on the basement floor as the early-warning system, and know that a soaked basement means the clock starts on <a href="/home/mould-after-a-flooded-room/">drying before mould</a> - with <a href="/home/hidden-water-leak-meter-test/">the meter trick</a> ready for the leaks that never trigger the pump at all. Ten seconds of bucket water per season is the cheapest basement insurance sold.</p>"""),

("ice-dam-prevention-checklist", "maintain",
"Ice dams: the checklist that keeps them off your roof",
"The melt-refreeze cycle that wrecks roofs in snow country - the three-part prevention (seal, insulate, ventilate) and what to never do with an axe.",
"""<p>An ice dam is a frozen ridge along the roof's edge - and the cause of some of winter's most expensive ceiling stains. The mechanics matter, because the fix is almost never up on the roof; it is in the attic.</p>
<h2>How a dam forms</h2>
<p>Heat escaping from the living space warms the roof deck; snow touching the warm upper roof melts and runs down until it reaches the cold eave - over the unheated overhang - where it refreezes. The ridge grows, and water pooling behind it has nowhere to go but <i>under</i> the shingles, into the loft, and eventually through the ceiling. The clue set: icicles the size of your arm, a bare patch of roof mid-winter while the eaves stay white, and damp patches appearing at ceiling edges after thaw cycles.</p>
<h2>The prevention checklist - all three, in order</h2>
<p><b>1. Seal the air leaks.</b> Warm air rising through ceiling penetrations - light fittings, hatches, wiring holes, chimneys - is the fuel. Sealing those gaps from the attic side is the highest-impact fix on this list.</p>
<p><b>2. Insulate the attic floor.</b> Keeping the heat in the house and out of the attic keeps the whole roof cold, so snow melts evenly (or not at all) instead of in patches.</p>
<p><b>3. Ventilate the attic.</b> Soffit-to-ridge airflow flushes residual heat out and keeps the deck at outdoor temperature. Seal plus insulate plus ventilate is the standard cold-roof recipe - and it also fixes <a href="/home/condensation-ventilation-that-works/">the condensation problems that share the same root</a>.</p>
<h2>Mid-winter measures (and the one that is banned)</h2>
<p>After heavy snow, a roof rake used <i>from the ground</i> pulls the first metre of snow off the eaves, denying the dam its raw material. Heated cables along the edge are a targeted patch for trouble spots - a treatment, not a cure. What is <b>never</b> on the list: chipping at the ice with axes or hammers (shingles lose), salt pelters dumped broadly (plants and metalwork lose), or climbing an icy roof (you lose). Significant dam or an active leak means a professional - this is ladders-and-ice territory, and the <a href="/home/basic-toolkit-checklist/">toolkit stays on the ground for this one</a>.</p>
<p>Spring follow-up: the dams of this winter are this spring's gutter inspection - <a href="/home/gutters-and-downpipes/">check the troughs and downpipes survived</a>, note <a href="/home/roof-gutter-leak-joint/">any joint leaks to reseal in dry weather</a>, and if ceilings stained anyway, dry them fast and watch for <a href="/home/mould-after-a-flooded-room/">the after-damp that follows water indoors</a>. The checklist costs a weekend in autumn; the dams cost a ceiling.</p>"""),
]
